# arvo. Architecture Design

> Numeric primitives + analysis algorithms with Hot / Warm / Cold /
> Precise strategy markers. `#![no_std]`, no alloc. Zero dependency
> on platform. Designed so every downstream consumer shares one
> numeric layer.

## Principles

1. **Strategy-parameterised primitives.** Numeric containers declared
   at type level as `Hot` / `Warm` / `Cold` / `Precise`, driving
   storage width and codegen choices without runtime dispatch.
2. **no_std, no alloc.** Every crate is const-sized.
   Consumers pre-allocate; arvo never calls into an allocator.
3. **Trait contracts, not concrete types.** Sub-crates generic over
   trait bounds so downstream code picks its own concrete
   instantiations. Trait declarations live in dedicated contract
   crates (small transitive dependency footprint per consumer).
4. **Monomorphisation is the dispatch.** No `dyn`, no `TypeId`. The
   monomorphised function IS the type identity.
5. **Const-trait-driven.** Every trait whose methods are sensibly
   callable in const context is `pub const trait` (RFC 3762 line).
   Trait projections work in const fn bodies; default bodies stay
   reachable from const generic positions.

## Crate topology

arvo splits into a layered set of small crates with a downstream
facade. Dependency direction runs strictly upward; the facade sits at
the bottom.

| Layer | Crate | What it ships |
|---|---|---|
| L0a | `arvo-transparent` | `Transparent` const unsafe trait. `NumericPrimitive` marker. Free `arvo::raw` const fn. |
| L0a | `arvo-strategy` | `Strategy` marker, `Hot` / `Warm` / `Cold` / `Precise` ZSTs, unified `BitsContainerFor<const N: u16, Sign>` const trait via Pattern C const-tag dispatch through `Project<TAG, Sign, BYTES, S>` helper, `Resolve<S1, S2>`, `Width` newtype. |
| L0  | `arvo-storage` | `Bits<N, S, Sign>` storage primitive (default `Sign = Unsigned`). `WideBits<BYTES>` byte-sequence (Warm/Cold/Precise above 128). `AlignedWideBits16<BYTES>` align(16) byte-sequence (Hot above 128). `Bool`, `USize` platform wrappers. `IBits`, `FBits` typed meta-bit newtypes (`Width` re-exported from arvo-strategy). |
| L0.5 | `arvo-bits-contracts` | `HasBitWidth`, `BitAccess`, `BitSequence`, `BitLogic`, `BitPrim`, `UBitContainer`, `IBitContainer`, `Narrow<T>`, `Widen<T>` (all `pub const trait`). Hosts the cross-primitive + cross-Bits Narrow / Widen impl tables. |
| L0.5 | `arvo-mask-contracts` | `MaskOps` const trait. |
| L0.5 | `arvo-numeric-contracts` | `Abs`, `Recip`, `Sqrt`, `TotalOrd`, `FromConstant`, `Predicate` family (all `pub const trait`). |
| L1  | `arvo-bits` | Concrete blanket impls of bit-level traits on `Bits<N, S>` and bare primitives. Bit-storage aliases (`Bit`, `Nibble`, `Byte`, `Word`, `DWord`, `QWord`). |
| L1  | `arvo-bitmask` | `Mask<B>` chassis, `BitMatrix<B, const N: Cap>` chassis. `MaskOps` impls at concrete widths 64 and 256. |
| L1  | `arvo-refit` | Re-export gateway for the bit-width refit family (`Narrow<T>`, `Widen<T>`, `Narrowed`, `Widened`). |
| L1  | `arvo-tensor` | `Array<T, N>`, `Matrix<W, N>`, `Cap`, `Enumerator`. |
| L2  | `arvo-hash` | `Hasher<N>` const trait, `Fnv1a<N>`, `ContentHash` alias. |
| L2  | `arvo-graph` | DAG algorithms. |
| L2  | `arvo-sparse` | CSR, RCM, block-diagonal, Dulmage-Mendelsohn. |
| L2  | `arvo-comb` | Combinatorial optimisation. |
| L3  | `arvo-spectral` | Laplacian, Fiedler, power iteration. |
| facade | `arvo` | Re-exports public surface. Hosts `UFixed<I, F, S>` and `IFixed<I, F, S>` as `repr(transparent)` newtypes over `Bits`. Defines `Int<const N: u16, S>` / `Uint<const N: u16, S>` ergonomic generic aliases. Hosts default impls of the numeric-contract traits. Floats (`FastFloat`, `StrictFloat`) and `Cap` live here too. |

## Trait-contract crates

The split into five contract crates (`arvo-transparent`,
`arvo-strategy`, `arvo-bits-contracts` (which hosts the bit-access,
narrow, and widen surfaces), `arvo-mask-contracts`,
`arvo-numeric-contracts`) optimises transitive dependency *size*,
not count. A consumer that only needs the bit-access surface depends
on `arvo-bits-contracts` plus `arvo-storage`, not on `arvo-bitmask`
or `arvo-tensor`. The implementation crates pull only what they
implement.

The split is a starting structure. Pairs of contract crates that
100% co-occur in real consumer dep sets merge over time.

## The arvo facade

`arvo` is the bottom of the dependency graph. Every other workspace
crate is upstream. The facade re-exports the public surface:
`Bits<N, S, Sign>`, the strategy markers, `Bool`, `USize`, `IBits` /
`FBits` / `Width`, the contract traits, the mask types, the narrow
trait, the hash types, the tensor types, the algorithm crate publics.

The facade itself defines:

- `UFixed<I, F, S>` and `IFixed<I, F, S>`: `repr(transparent)`
  newtypes over `Bits<{I+F}, S, Unsigned>` and
  `Bits<{1+I+F}, S, Signed>`. Arithmetic via const trait impls of
  `Add` / `Sub` / `Mul` / `Div` / `Recip` / `Sqrt`, not via direct
  field manipulation.
- `Int<const N: u16, S = Warm>` and `Uint<const N: u16, S = Warm>`:
  ergonomic generic aliases. `N` is total storage bit count.
  Consumers reach for the exact width they need (`Uint<3>`,
  `Uint<13>`, `Int<47>`); std-parallel `Uint8`/`Int8`/etc.
  spellings are not part of the surface.
- `FastFloat`, `StrictFloat`.
- `Cap`.
- Default impls of the numeric-contract traits over the composed
  types.

## Dependency direction

Strictly upward. Each lower-layer crate names only crates above it
(or no workspace deps for L0a). The facade names every other crate.

## Consumers

arvo is designed to be consumed by:

- **hilavitkutin**: pipeline execution engine. Uses `arvo-graph` for
  plan-stage DAG analysis, `arvo-bitmask` for `AccessMask`,
  `arvo-sparse` for RCM reordering, `arvo-spectral` for trunk
  formation, `arvo-comb` for fiber grouping.
- **vehje** (language stack): uses `arvo-graph` for pass DAG,
  `arvo-bitmask` for artifact read/write sets, `arvo-hash` for
  content fingerprints.
- Other downstream applications: miscellaneous numeric needs plus
  whatever hilavitkutin transitively exposes.

